From ccc5fce7cf3a6325ca294d6a2d4d5bff1f7a9d8f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lakr <git@qaq.wiki>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 08:04:30 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 15/24] Condemn a surface the guest unwired, and drop the swaps
 it cancelled

On 27.2 the first display swap waits behind a render that takes 18 s to
translate and compile; after five seconds IOMFB cancels its swaps and
releases the swap buffer once too often, so the surface descriptor of a
still-mapped mapping drops to a wire count of zero. Its pages then go
back to the kernel while this device still holds the mapping.

With REIMS_VGPU_WIRE_GUARD, the watch's first anomaly on a mapping now
condemns that incarnation (wire_guard_condemned) instead of refusing
only while a count reads zero. For as long as the MappingInternal is the
same:

- every host write into it is refused, CPU and GPU-direct alike;
- the frame owed to it is dropped when it is condemned, and any later
  debt is dropped at payment rather than put back;
- a display present naming it that was submitted before the drop was
  seen is completed without running (wire_guard_swap_dropped): its stamp
  is written, nothing is shown and no present event is raised. The
  horizon is each child FIFO's published tail when the drop was seen;
- none of this is undone if a count turns positive again
  (wire_guard_still_condemned).

The resident is kept, so a later present of the same surface, submitted
after the drop, still shows it without touching guest memory.

A swap seen waiting at a held FIFO's head now puts its surface on the
display sampling cadence, so the drop is seen within 5 ms rather than at
the next 100 ms pass.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
(cherry picked from commit 6e56f44514ffcccadc4e8f2a9c7eff83c97a1ee0)
